Natas Level 7: Local File Inclusion (LFI) / Path Traversal
What You See
A simple page with "Home" and "About" links
URL structure: index.php?page=home
The Vulnerability
The page parameter in the URL is used to include a file on the server. This is a classic Local File Inclusion (LFI) vulnerability.
When you click "Home", the URL becomes:
plain
index.php?page=home
The server does something like:
php
<?php
include($_GET['page'] . ".php");
?>
The Exploit: Path Traversal
Since the server includes whatever file you specify, you can traverse the filesystem to read sensitive files.
Target: The password file for the next level is stored at:
plain
/etc/natas_webpass/natas8
Payload:
plain
http://natas7.natas.labs.overthewire.org/index.php?page=/etc/natas_webpass/natas8
Why It Works
The page parameter accepts any file path
The server doesn't validate or sanitize the input
The .php extension might be appended, but /etc/natas_webpass/natas8 is read as a valid path regardless
The file contents are dumped directly into the page
Password for Natas Level 8
plain
ugXL95KQmUAJJj6bMezOlBNDyI9Imwkc
Key Takeaways
Table
Lesson	Explanation
Never trust user input in file paths	Always validate and whitelist allowed files
Path traversal with ../ or absolute paths	Attackers can escape the intended directory
Sensitive files in predictable locations	/etc/natas_webpass/natasX is a known target
LFI can lead to RCE	In some cases, LFI can be escalated to Remote Code Execution
